+
+If you already have this checkout, use its project directory. Build and install:
+
+```sh
+flatpak-builder --user --install-deps-from=flathub --install builddir io.github.nokse22.teleprompter.json
+flatpak run io.github.nokse22.teleprompter
+```
+
+The manifest uses the **GNOME 49 SDK/runtime**. Flatpak Builder can install these
+dependencies from Flathub. This avoids relying on Mint's system GTK/libadwaita
+versions for the app's recent widgets.
+
+Use an empty `builddir` for the first build. For subsequent builds, add
+`--force-clean` to the `flatpak-builder` command to replace that build directory.
+
+### Alternative: GNOME Builder
+
+Open this project folder in GNOME Builder, select its Flatpak build
+configuration, and click **Run Project**. Allow Builder to install the GNOME 49
+SDK/runtime if prompted.
+
+## Play an SRT file
+
+1. Launch the app.
+2. Click the **folder button** or press **Ctrl+O**, then select your `.srt` file.
+   To try the included example, choose [examples/timed-demo.srt](examples/timed-demo.srt).
+3. The file opens **paused at 00:00:00.000**, with its timing offset set to zero.
+   The smaller **Up next** preview lets you prepare for the first cue.
+4. Click **Play** or press **Ctrl+Space**. The timeline starts at zero and each
+   cue becomes active at its SRT start time.
+5. Press **Ctrl+Space** again to pause. Press it once more to resume from the
+   same position.
+6. Playback stops at the last cue's end. Press **Play** again to start over.
+
+Open the file through the app's file chooser; passing an SRT filename to the
+launch command is not currently supported.
+
+### What you see
+
+- **Current cue:** large, bold text, visible from its start time until its end time.
+- **Up next:** the next cue in smaller text. This preview is visible before that
+  cue's start time, including during gaps.
+- **Status and timeline:** playback state, elapsed/total time, and the current
+  cue number or a countdown to the next cue.
+
+When cues overlap, all active cues appear together in their original file order.
+When a cue ends, its current-cue text clears; during a gap, the next-cue preview
+remains available.
 
-You just need to clone the repository
+### Controls
+
+| Control | Action |
+| --- | --- |
+| **Ctrl+O** / folder button | Open an SRT or text file. |
+| **Ctrl+Space** / Play button | Play or pause. |
+| **Timeline slider** | Seek to a different time. |
+| **Alt+Left** / previous button | Jump to the preceding cue start; from inside a cue, return to that cue's start first. |
+| **Alt+Right** / next button | Jump to the next cue's start. |
+| **Ctrl+Home** / restart button | Pause and return to zero. |
+| **Offset (s)** | Shift when cues appear, in steps of 0.1 seconds. |
+| **Font buttons / Preferences** | Adjust readability without changing cue timing. |
+| **F11** | Toggle fullscreen. |
+
+Seeking and cue navigation preserve the playing/paused state unless you reach
+the end of the script.
+
+### Adjust the timing offset
+
+Positive offsets show cues **later**; negative offsets show them **earlier**.
+For example, a cue beginning at 5 seconds appears at 7 seconds with an offset of
+`+2.0`, or at 3 seconds with an offset of `-2.0`.
+
+Changing the offset shifts the cue schedule without moving the current timeline
+position or editing the file. A negative offset can move initial cues before
+zero, so those cues may be partly or completely skipped. Restart retains the
+offset; opening another SRT resets it to zero.
+
+## SRT format and text handling
+
+A simple file looks like this. Leave a blank line between cues:
+
+```srt
+1
+00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:05,000
+Welcome to the lecture.
+
+2
+00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:10,000
+This cue contains
+two lines of text.
+```
+
+UTF-8, UTF-8 with a BOM, and UTF-16 with a BOM are supported. Save files in other
+encodings as UTF-8 before opening them. Invalid files produce an error identifying
+the cue/line where possible and leave the current document and playback intact.
+
+The app preserves Unicode, wording, and multiline cues. Common presentation tags
+(`<b>`, `<i>`, `<u>`, `<s>`, and `<font>`) are hidden in the display,
+`<br>` becomes a line break, and HTML entities such as `&amp;` display as
+characters. The source file is never modified.
+
+SRT playback runs at **1×**. Font size, window size, mirroring, and the normal
+reading-speed setting do not affect the timestamps. The app uses elapsed
+monotonic time to avoid accumulating drift when UI updates run late; visible
+updates occur approximately every 20 ms while playing.
+
+The displayed SRT text is read-only. To change the wording or timestamps, edit
+the source file separately and reopen it.
+
+## Return to ordinary scrolling text
+
+Open a normal text file or click **Paste from Clipboard**. This returns to
+editable, speed-based scrolling. Clipboard paste always uses plain-text mode;
+open an SRT file to activate timed playback.
+
+## Run the automated checks
+
+The parser, timeline, and controller tests use Python's standard library. They
+can run without GTK, a display server, or extra Python packages:
 
 ```sh
-git clone https://github.com/Nokse22/teleprompter.git
+python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -v
+python3 -m compileall -q src tests
+git diff --check
 ```
 
-Open the project in GNOME Builder and click "Run Project".
+The controller tests use GTK test doubles. Run the actual Flatpak app to check
+widget layout and rendering on your Linux desktop.
